WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.060 line:10%,align:center
Your next favourite skincare ingredient
may have been invented by bacteria

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.391 line:10%,align:center
trying not to die.

00:00:05.753 --> 00:00:06.804 line:10%,align:center
Not snail mucin.

00:00:06.804 --> 00:00:08.242 line:10%,align:center
Not salmon DNA.

00:00:08.242 --> 00:00:11.212 line:10%,align:center
And not another form of hyaluronic acid.

00:00:11.362 --> 00:00:16.062 line:-3,align:center
This tiny molecule helps microorganisms
survive places so salty and so

00:00:16.242 --> 00:00:20.022 line:-3,align:center
dry that an ordinary cell would
lose its water and stop functioning.

00:00:20.172 --> 00:00:22.482 line:-3,align:center
And it is now turning up,

00:00:22.482 --> 00:00:26.044 line:-3,align:center
quietly, in Korean serums,
creams and sunscreens.

00:00:26.044 --> 00:00:29.304 line:-3,align:center
It is called ectoin, and the
real science is stranger than

00:00:29.304 --> 00:00:30.534 line:-3,align:center
the marketing.

00:00:31.184 --> 00:00:33.988 line:-3,align:center
Ectoin is what researchers
call an extremolyte.

00:00:33.988 --> 00:00:35.466 line:-3,align:center
That sounds like a superhero.

00:00:35.466 --> 00:00:40.516 line:-3,align:center
It just means a protective molecule
made by microbes living somewhere extreme.

00:00:40.766 --> 00:00:44.076 line:-3,align:center
The bacterium we get most of
it from has a name.

00:00:44.076 --> 00:00:46.051 line:-3,align:center
Halomonas elongata.

00:00:46.051 --> 00:00:47.133 line:-3,align:center
It lives in salt.

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:49.373 line:-3,align:center
When the salt outside gets punishing,

00:00:49.373 --> 00:00:51.163 line:-3,align:center
it floods itself with ectoin.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:52.417 line:-3,align:center
Why?

00:00:52.437 --> 00:00:53.966 line:-3,align:center
Because salt pulls water out of cells.

00:00:53.966 --> 00:00:56.917 line:-3,align:center
Lose enough water and proteins
start to lose their shape,

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.759 line:-3,align:center
membranes get unstable, and
the cell stops working.

00:01:01.009 --> 00:01:04.780 line:10%,align:center
Ectoin keeps the space around
those fragile structures survivable.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.633 line:10%,align:center
Which led skincare researchers
to an obvious question.

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:10.433 line:10%,align:center
Could it do the same
for stressed human skin?

00:01:11.433 --> 00:01:15.333 line:-3,align:center
Here is where ectoin stops
behaving like a normal moisturiser.

00:01:16.043 --> 00:01:20.266 line:-3,align:center
Familiar humectants, such as
glycerin and hyaluronic acid,

00:01:20.266 --> 00:01:23.281 line:-3,align:center
attract water and hold on to it.

00:01:23.281 --> 00:01:28.361 line:-3,align:center
Ectoin seems to affect how water arranges
itself around proteins and membranes.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.649 line:-3,align:center
Scientists call the
idea preferential exclusion.

00:01:31.682 --> 00:01:32.935 line:-3,align:center
The tidy version.

00:01:32.935 --> 00:01:36.420 line:-3,align:center
Ectoin protects proteins by
staying off their surface,

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.595 line:-3,align:center
which helps the water
around them stay organised.

00:01:40.016 --> 00:01:42.548 line:-3,align:center
The honest version is messier.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.398 line:-3,align:center
In the simulations, ectoin also showed
some attraction to that surface.

00:01:48.398 --> 00:01:51.941 line:-3,align:center
And how far it stays back
depends on how tightly the

00:01:51.941 --> 00:01:55.387 line:-3,align:center
protein's own water is already arranged.

00:01:55.537 --> 00:01:58.726 line:-3,align:center
Think of a celebrity inside
a ring of security.

00:01:59.298 --> 00:02:02.165 line:-3,align:center
Ectoin is not hugging the celebrity.

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:06.510 line:-3,align:center
It hangs back, and helps
the ring hold its shape.

00:02:06.516 --> 00:02:12.618 line:-3,align:center
Which is why calling it another hydrating
ingredient misses the interesting part.

00:02:13.168 --> 00:02:16.078 line:10%,align:center
Your outer skin barrier
is under constant load.

00:02:16.108 --> 00:02:19.407 line:10%,align:center
Dry air, cleansing, pollution,
sun, strong actives.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.016 line:10%,align:center
When it struggles, water
escapes more easily,

00:02:22.089 --> 00:02:23.959 line:10%,align:center
and skin can feel tight, rough,

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:25.789 line:10%,align:center
or unusually reactive.

00:02:25.939 --> 00:02:31.552 line:-3,align:center
Lab research suggests ectoin can
help stabilise biological structures and

00:02:31.552 --> 00:02:35.305 line:-3,align:center
improve hydration in that outer layer.

00:02:35.305 --> 00:02:38.021 line:-3,align:center
One study found it changed how keratin,

00:02:38.195 --> 00:02:41.095 line:-3,align:center
the main protein in our outer skin cells,

00:02:41.095 --> 00:02:42.672 line:-3,align:center
behaves with water.

00:02:42.822 --> 00:02:46.243 line:10%,align:center
Though in that same
study, in punishing dryness,

00:02:46.243 --> 00:02:50.633 line:10%,align:center
below five percent humidity, one
stress measure actually got worse.

00:02:50.643 --> 00:02:52.809 line:10%,align:center
It is not a force field.

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:56.599 line:-3,align:center
It is better understood as a
supporting ingredient that may help

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:00.779 line:-3,align:center
skin handle dehydration and
environmental stress more comfortably.

00:03:01.229 --> 00:03:05.873 line:-3,align:center
So does it do anything to actual people?

00:03:05.873 --> 00:03:09.368 line:-3,align:center
In one randomised, double-blind study,

00:03:09.368 --> 00:03:12.668 line:-3,align:center
a hundred and four women used
a cream with two percent

00:03:12.668 --> 00:03:15.993 line:-3,align:center
ectoin against the same cream without it.

00:03:16.193 --> 00:03:18.267 line:-3,align:center
On the study's own measure,

00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:23.929 line:-3,align:center
the women themselves preferred the ectoin
version. That is a real result.

00:03:23.929 --> 00:03:25.952 line:-3,align:center
And it is a preference,

00:03:25.952 --> 00:03:29.106 line:-3,align:center
not a machine reading their skin.

00:03:29.256 --> 00:03:34.493 line:-3,align:center
A second randomised study followed sixty
five people with mild to

00:03:34.493 --> 00:03:36.563 line:-3,align:center
moderate atopic dermatitis, eczema.

00:03:36.563 --> 00:03:40.549 line:-3,align:center
Over four weeks, an ectoin cream
performed about as well as

00:03:40.602 --> 00:03:43.535 line:-3,align:center
the barrier cream it was tested against,

00:03:43.535 --> 00:03:46.340 line:-3,align:center
and was well tolerated.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:47.490 line:10%,align:center
Encouraging.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:49.465 line:10%,align:center
Not proof that ectoin cures eczema,

00:03:49.702 --> 00:03:53.697 line:10%,align:center
reverses ageing, or replaces
anything a dermatologist prescribes.

00:03:54.397 --> 00:03:56.839 line:-3,align:center
And the evidence base is genuinely small.

00:03:56.839 --> 00:04:02.813 line:-3,align:center
Search PubMed today and you will find
twelve ectoin clinical trials. Twelve.

00:04:03.713 --> 00:04:08.341 line:10%,align:center
Some of that research comes from
companies that sell the ingredient.

00:04:08.341 --> 00:04:09.614 line:10%,align:center
bitop.

00:04:09.614 --> 00:04:12.306 line:10%,align:center
Merck. And Kao. So.

00:04:12.324 --> 00:04:15.450 line:10%,align:center
Promising supporting ingredient, yes.

00:04:15.729 --> 00:04:17.239 line:10%,align:center
Miracle molecule, no.

00:04:17.689 --> 00:04:20.716 line:-3,align:center
Ectoin is most interesting if
your skin runs dry,

00:04:20.745 --> 00:04:24.187 line:-3,align:center
sensitive, over-cleansed, or irritated
by a strong routine.

00:04:24.209 --> 00:04:26.434 line:-3,align:center
It sits comfortably alongside panthenol,

00:04:26.434 --> 00:04:28.651 line:-3,align:center
glycerin, squalane and ceramides.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.223 line:10%,align:center
But do not buy it off
the front of the bottle.

00:04:32.223 --> 00:04:33.867 line:10%,align:center
Turn it around.

00:04:33.867 --> 00:04:37.072 line:10%,align:center
Plenty of brands do print a number.

00:04:38.047 --> 00:04:40.082 line:10%,align:center
Paula's Choice says seven percent.

00:04:40.459 --> 00:04:43.657 line:10%,align:center
The Ordinary lists two percent ectoin.

00:04:43.968 --> 00:04:48.568 line:10%,align:center
The brand Abib promotes
Ectoin Panthenol eleven percent,

00:04:48.835 --> 00:04:51.857 line:10%,align:center
but that eleven percent
combines the two ingredients.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.229 line:10%,align:center
Panthenol is second on
the ingredient list.

00:04:55.229 --> 00:04:57.389 line:10%,align:center
Ectoin is eleventh.

00:04:57.389 --> 00:05:01.515 line:10%,align:center
The big number on the front
is not always the ectoin number.

00:05:01.765 --> 00:05:04.575 line:-3,align:center
The rest is judgement, not evidence.

00:05:04.575 --> 00:05:07.915 line:-3,align:center
Is it fragrance-free, if
fragrance bothers you?

00:05:07.915 --> 00:05:11.106 line:-3,align:center
Does the formula carry
other useful moisturisers?

00:05:11.106 --> 00:05:14.376 line:-3,align:center
One good ingredient cannot rescue
a badly built product.

00:05:14.826 --> 00:05:17.882 line:-3,align:center
K-beauty did not invent ectoin.

00:05:17.882 --> 00:05:21.182 line:-3,align:center
What Korean formulators are doing well
is pairing it with barrier

00:05:21.762 --> 00:05:23.972 line:-3,align:center
ingredients in light, wearable textures.

00:05:24.222 --> 00:05:29.040 line:-3,align:center
That fits the wider move away
from aggressive transformation and towards

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.838 line:-3,align:center
skin that simply stays comfortable.

00:05:31.838 --> 00:05:34.690 line:-3,align:center
You will already find it
in Korean barrier serums,

00:05:34.769 --> 00:05:39.321 line:-3,align:center
toners and sun products, often in
smaller type than the trend suggests.

00:05:39.821 --> 00:05:42.974 line:10%,align:center
So next time you see
ectoin on an ingredient list,

00:05:43.194 --> 00:05:44.841 line:10%,align:center
remember what you are looking at.

00:05:44.841 --> 00:05:48.003 line:10%,align:center
A survival strategy,
borrowed from bacteria.

00:05:48.003 --> 00:05:50.456 line:10%,align:center
Not the new hyaluronic
acid. Not a miracle.

00:05:50.456 --> 00:05:53.817 line:10%,align:center
A genuinely interesting
supporting molecule.

00:05:54.717 --> 00:05:56.892 line:10%,align:center
Here is the one thing worth doing.

00:05:56.892 --> 00:06:01.042 line:10%,align:center
Turn the bottle around and find
the actual percentage before you buy.

00:06:01.042 --> 00:06:05.410 line:10%,align:center
And tell me, would you
put a bacteria-made survival molecule

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:07.092 line:10%,align:center
on your face?

00:06:08.492 --> 00:06:10.330 line:-3,align:center
Follow SeoulHabit for more.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:11.630 line:-3,align:center
[soft music fades]
